<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tazeroualt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tazeroualt is a region in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-atlas">Anti-Atlas</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a> valley. It holds the shrine of the 16th-century saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidi-ahmed-ou-moussa">Sidi Ahmed ou Moussa</a>, and in the 17th century his descendants made Iligh the capital of a sharifate that controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and the Atlantic coast. The family remained influential local lords into the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tazeroualt.jpg" alt="Tazeroualt" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucyin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zawiya_Sidi_Ahmed_ou_Moussa_tombe-tchapele_payizaedje_Tazerwalt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tazerwalt lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-atlas">Anti-Atlas</a> mountains of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, and its name is also spelled Tazeroualt. Sidi Ahmed Ou Moussa, a marabout from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Souss</a>, settled in the area and founded a zawiya there, where hundreds of followers came to hear his teaching. He kept close relations with the ruling Saadian dynasty and used his religious standing to carve out an enclave of power within the Saadian state. After his death his grave became a place of pilgrimage, and his descendants inherited much of his wealth and status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The death of the Saadian sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> in 1603 threw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> into instability, and Sidi Ahmed Ou Moussa's grandson Sidi Ali Bou Dmia turned the family's religious standing into political power. Calling himself amir of Tazerwalt, he founded his capital at Iligh and bound the region to him through his religious following and tribal alliances. Holding overland trade routes and ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a>, he raised a sizable army, mainly of slaves, and pushed his borders east. By the mid-17th century he ruled a wide stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and of Morocco south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a>, including the trading centres of the Draa valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>, Touat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taghaza">Taghaza</a>, and at its height the state reached from the Atlantic to Touat.</p><p>The rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouite dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> undid this. In the late 1650s the Alaouites took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> and cut away much of Tazerwalt's economic power. Sidi Ali Bou Dmia died in 1659 and was briefly followed by his son Muhammad, and in 1670 the Alaouites razed Iligh, ending the state's independence.</p><p>The family kept wealth and prestige as the House of Iligh through its descent from the saint. Without ever challenging Alaouite rule directly, it regained much political and even military power in the 18th and 19th centuries, controlling much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Souss</a> and dealing in trade and diplomacy with both the Alaouite sultans and European powers. Sidi Ahmed Ou Moussa's zawiya is still a place of pilgrimage, in the village that bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tazerwalt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Across Morocco regional powers filled the vacuum: the marabout al-Ayyashi waged his own war against the Spanish enclaves, the Sharifate of <b>Tazeroualt</b> ruled the Sus, the Dila’iyya brotherhood came to dominate the Middle Atlas and Fez, and Morisco corsairs founded the Republic of Salé in 1627.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Hassan Ali al-Semlali, a descendant of the saint Sidi Ahmed ou Moussa, built a power base in the <b>Tazeroualt</b> region of the Anti-Atlas.</blockquote><a href="/state/sharifate-of-tazeroualt">Sharifate of Tazeroualt</a></li></ul></section>
